.TH CTHREADCOMM 7 "2026-10-06" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Overview"
.SH NAME
cthreadcomm \- overview of the thread communication API
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadcomm.h>
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
The module moves data between threads. It also waits until a queue or
a file descriptor is ready. It has five parts:

  ccol_circular_queue   A bounded queue. A send blocks while the queue
                        is full. This gives backpressure to a producer.
  ccol_dynamic_queue    An unbounded queue. A send never waits.
  ccol_channel          Two circular queues, one in each direction.
                        The identity of the calling thread selects
                        the queue.
  ccol_select()         A one-shot wait for the first ready item among
                        several queues or file descriptors.
  ccol_event_loop       A persistent reactor. It runs callbacks for a
                        set of queues and file descriptors that can
                        change.
.fi
.SS Messages and ownership
.nf
Every queue carries c_message_t values:

  typedef struct c_message_t {
      void   *data;
      size_t  size;
  } c_message_t;

The library copies no payload. A send that succeeds gives the
ownership of data to the queue. The library then sets the data field of
the sender to NULL. A receive gives the ownership to the receiver. The
receiver must free data. A send that fails does not change the message.
The caller therefore keeps the ownership of data. A message with a NULL data
and a size of 0 is valid. It is the usual end-of-stream sentinel.
.fi
.SS Blocking, try and timed calls
.nf
A plain send or receive blocks. A try_ call never blocks. It gives
ccol_container_full or ccol_container_empty. A timed_ call takes a
uint64_t count of microseconds. The library measures this time on
CLOCK_MONOTONIC. A change to the wall clock therefore does not make the
wait longer or shorter. With a timeout of 0, the timed call operates as
its try_ variant and gives the result of that variant. A timeout can be
too large for a deadline, for example UINT64_MAX. The library then uses
the largest deadline. The deadline never wraps into the past.
.fi
.SS Readiness only
.nf
ccol_select() and ccol_event_loop only tell that an item is ready. They
never receive or send. The caller then immediately makes its own call
that does not block:
  - For a queue: ccol_circq_try_recv_zc(), ccol_dynmq_try_recv_zc(),
    ccol_circq_try_send_zc() or ccol_dynmq_send_zc().
  - For a file descriptor: read(2), recv(2), write(2) or send(2).
A different consumer or producer can get the message or the space
before that call, as with POSIX select(2). Therefore, that call must not
block. Always examine its result.

An event loop registration can watch a queue. If that registration has
a pending wake, a send to the queue makes no system call. One dispatch
serves all the sends that came before the dispatch started.
.fi
.SS Handles
.nf
Queues and channels are plain pointers. Their destroy macros set the
pointer to NULL. ccol_event_loop and ccol_event_reg are opaque VALUE
handles (uint64_t), and not pointers. Compare them with
CCOL_EVENT_LOOP_INVALID and CCOL_EVENT_REG_INVALID. Both values are 0.
Before the library uses the object of such a handle, it resolves the
handle. The library therefore refuses a stale handle, and never
dereferences it.
.fi
.SS Process exit
.nf
The dispatch of each event loop uses two thread-specific keys. The
library creates these keys one time for each process. Queues and
channels need no key. If the process has no free key, the creation of
a loop fails with CCOL_EVENT_LOOP_INVALID for the rest of that process.
Queues and channels continue to operate.

The process-exit destructors of the library delete the keys. They also
free the bookkeeping of the library for all loops and queues. If a
queue or a loop is live at that time, its own destroy does this
release later. Therefore, an application destructor that runs after the
destructors of the library can destroy the objects that it owns. After
that release, the creation of a queue, a channel or a loop fails
cleanly.
.fi
.SS fork(2)
.nf
The library supports two ways to use fork(2) with this module:
  - Call fork(2) before you create a queue, a channel or a loop.
  - Create the object. Then call fork(2) and immediately exec(3).
The library does not support a child that continues with no exec(3)
while an object from before the fork is live. A queue has no thread of
its own. Therefore, a child never gets a locked queue from the parent. But an
event loop from the parent has no reactor threads in the child. It
dispatches nothing in the child.
.fi
